It is well known that one root is $e^{\frac{2\pi i}{3}}$. Since the triangle formed by two roots and the origin has three equal sides, $a$ and $b$ must be some variation of $(1,0), (0,1)$ or $(\frac{1}{2}, \frac{1}{2})$. Now, we use Bezout's Theorem. $\begin{vmatrix}x&y\\a&b\end{vmatrix} = ax - by$ gives all the possible combinations of $a$ and $b$ with $|z^3| = 1$. \begin{align*}\text{(1,0)}&\implies|3z| = 1\text{, but this is impossible}\\ \text{(0,1)}&\implies|-2z^2| = 1\\\text{($\frac{1}{2}, \frac{1}{2})$}&\implies \sqrt{\frac{3}{2}}\\ \end{align*}> Therefore, we are left with $(\sum_{i=0}^2\begin{vmatrix} x&y \\ a_{0i}&b_{0i} \end{vmatrix})\times(\sum_{j=0}^2\begin{vmatrix} x&y \\ a_{1j}&b_{1j} \end{vmatrix}) \\ = 9\times 9 = 81 \\\textbf{Answer}= 81\end{align*}
